 {"id":"BENCH-1","title":"Publish the LLM-core benchmark: run --decision-mode llm head-to-head with the fast tier and keep the no-JEV path permanently runnable","status":"complete","priority":"P1","complexity":3,"depends_on":[],"blocks":null,"primary_model":null,"primary_provider":null,"fallback_model":null,"fallback_provider":null,"reasoning":"Operator requirement (explicit): the project's original contribution is the LLM-core benchmark \u2014 what a reasoning model can do with NOTHING but the harness that gives it control of the game. The JEV tier became the default path and demoted that benchmark to an incidental fallback, so it could no longer be run or measured on its own terms. The selection switch now exists (--decision-mode jev|llm, resolved flag > AIPP_DECISION_MODE / CRON_DECISION_MODE > default; in llm mode the fast tier is never CALLED, not merely ignored; mode stamped into every decision row and the autonomy row). What remains is to make the benchmark real as a benchmark, not just runnable: (1) a bench runner that executes both modes from the SAME boot state for the SAME cycle budget and emits one comparable row per arm (mode, decisions, cost_usd, tiles visited, maps reached, map-transition cycle, lock-rate, fallback decisions, errors); (2) a published side-by-side artefact so 'can an LLM play Pokemon on its own' has a number attached and is re-runnable on demand; (3) a guard that fails a benchmark-labelled run if any row carries jev_answered=True, so assisted play can never be mistaken for the benchmark. Acceptance: one command reproduces both arms and the comparison table is regenerated from their logs alone, with no hand-entered figures.","created_at":"2026-09-26 00:40:00","worker_status":"complete","completed_at":"2026-09-29T22:19:22.963320Z","commit_hash":"3696fbc16c970715ebf41ef6e58cc8ee83bd","files_changed":["cron_runner.py","docs/reports/BENCH-1-head-to-head-benchmark.md","scripts/bench1_gen_smoke_fixture.py","scripts/bench1_red_evidence.py","scripts/benchmark_llm_vs_jev.py","tests/test_benchmark_llm_vs_jev.py","tests/test_benchmark_llm_vs_jev_driver.py"],"lines_added":1981,"lines_removed":16,"guard_result":"PASS","ci_result":"GREEN","worker_summary":"Verification-only closeout: BENCH-1 implementation is already merged at 3696fbc; 39 focused benchmark tests pass; latest three GitHub runs are successful, including exact-SHA CI for 3696fbc.","foreman_note":"No worker dispatched: implementation was already at HEAD and origin/main. GitReins BENCH-1 task completed separately; Tier 2 verdict pending at closeout write.","updated_at":"2026-09-29T22:19:22.963320Z"}
-{"id":"AGENT-1","title":"Give the agent a real agentic context window, real tool calls, and the ability to delegate research to a subagent","status":"pending","priority":"P1","complexity":5,"depends_on":[],"blocks":null,"primary_model":null,"primary_provider":null,"fallback_model":null,"fallback_provider":null,"reasoning":"MEASURED GAP (operator question, 2026-09-26): 'is the agent getting turns and past events like a coding-agent context window, and is it wired to make tool calls so it can think, decide, and delegate research?' Answer from the code: NO, not yet. (1) CONTEXT: every cycle is a fresh single-turn request - controller_plan builds messages=[system, user] and nothing else (cron_runner.py ~1744). There is no message history and no persisted transcript anywhere. The past survives only as (a) _mem_notes capped at 6 entries and a single _mem_goal string (cron_runner.py 3091-3092, joined and truncated to 300 chars at 3577), (b) boot_memory injected at boot (MEM-2), and (c) an in-run `results` list handed to JEV/teacher as recent_events - an event log, not a context window. So the agent cannot see its own recent reasoning or re-read text it consumed. (2) TOOLS: src/core/ai_client.py DOES support native OpenAI function calling (tools param 562, payload wiring 595-599, tool_calls parsing 710-713, text-only model fallback 861-899) - but the controller path never uses it: the controller returns a JSON plan (a list of button presses). _battle_tool_call looks like tool use but is a FIXED translation table from JEV battle vocabulary (MOVE_n/RUN/SWITCH) to emulator transport calls - the model is not choosing tools. (3) DELEGATION: grep for subagent/delegate/research finds NOTHING. ACCEPTANCE: (a) each cycle carries a BOUNDED rolling context window (last N turns plus a compressed running summary) so the agent sees its own recent decisions, results, and the text it read; (b) the controller can emit REAL tool calls that the harness executes (walk/interact/read-dialogue/query-memory/look-up) instead of only emitting a button plan; (c) the agent can DELEGATE: spawn a bounded research subagent for an online lookup and receive a compressed finding back into its context and DuckBrain; (d) all of it cost-capped, bounded, and stamped per decision-mode so the pure-LLM benchmark (BENCH-1) stays comparable; (e) EVIDENCE = one run whose log shows at least one model-CHOSEN tool call and at least one delegated research result that is consumed in a LATER cycle.","created_at":"2026-09-26 01:05:00"}
